<p>This playbook is for practitioners and teams codifying how they work so that the practice transfers, improves, and retires. It treats playbook creation as a system with a lifecycle rather than a writing exercise, grounded in Toyota Kata, standard work, Argyris's theory-in-use, and pattern language thinking.</p>

{% marpDeck "content/playbooks/creating-playbooks/overview.marp.md", "/playbooks/creating-playbooks/overview/" %}

<p>The playbook is structured in nine parts:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/core-thesis/">The Core Thesis</a></strong> - codification standardises, improvement requires variation, and the playbook only survives if it resolves that tension.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/what-a-playbook-is/">What a Playbook Is</a></strong> - plays as situated moves, and why a playbook cannot codify your culture.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/sense/">Sense</a></strong> - noticing practice worth codifying, and the discipline of not codifying too early.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/extract/">Extract</a></strong> - getting the play from what happened rather than from the official story.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/codify/">Codify</a></strong> - entry conditions, forces, signals, and the when-not-to that makes a play a play.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/publish/">Publish</a></strong> - publishing before you are confident, and findability as a situation problem rather than a taxonomy problem.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/format-and-access/">Format and Access</a></strong> - public by default, org-wide as the floor, and why neither reading nor proposing may depend on technical wizardry.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/govern/">Govern</a></strong> - the local-improvement-to-new-standard loop that the whole system exists to serve, and retirement as a first-class outcome.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/open-questions/">Open Questions</a></strong> - solo versus organisational collections, evidence thresholds, and what a play library owes its forks.</li>
</ol>

<p>Each lifecycle stage pairs its plays with the antipattern they exist to prevent. Elements of the playbook are hypotheses rather than proven positions. They are marked as such throughout.</p>

<p>The <strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/artefacts/">Artefacts</a></strong> section contains the operational tools that put the playbook into action: the <em>Is It a Playbook</em> test for auditing a draft or an existing collection, the Play Template for writing new plays in a consistent form, Worked Examples running the test against well-known playbooks in the wild, the <a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/overview/">intro slides</a> (a projection, per the format rules), and a 90-minute back-of-the-room training session for remote delivery over Teams and Mural.</p>

